// The ShapeType of the OMG `dds-security` interoperability test suite —
// `srcCxx/ShapeType.idl`, pinned suite 62f5ec42.
//
// THERE ARE THREE ShapeTypes UNDER THIS NAME, and this is one of them.
// The other two are `omg-dds-rtps.idl` and `shapesdemo.idl` beside this
// file. Read `README.md` here before you use any of them.
//
// Who speaks this one
//   The executables of the OMG `dds-security` suite — the RTI,
//   CycloneDDS, Fast DDS, OpenDDS, CoreDX, Intercom and Vortex builds
//   of `srcCxx/ShapeType_main.cxx` that the suite drives against each
//   other — and `tools/omg-security-main` on our side. It is a
//   different suite from `dds-rtps` and a different type, even though
//   both call it `ShapeType`.
//
// Why it is not `omg-dds-rtps.idl`
//   Four differences, all of them verbatim from the suite's file:
//     1. The string bound is the named constant `COLOR_MAX_LENGTH` = 64,
//        not the literal 128 of the `dds-rtps` form.
//     2. There are four members. The fifth member of the `dds-rtps`
//        form, `sequence<uint8> additional_payload_size`, does not
//        exist here — this suite has no large-data run to fill it.
//     3. There is NO extensibility annotation. XTypes 1.3 §7.3.1.2.1
//        makes the absent annotation APPENDABLE, which is what
//        `tools/omg-security-main/build.rs` asks for; each vendor's own
//        IDL compiler applies its own default to this same file, and
//        the suite does not say which. That divergence is unmeasured
//        here and is written down in `README.md`.
//     4. The key is written `//@key` in a trailing comment, not as the
//        `@key` annotation of IDL 4.2 §7.4.14.
//
// The `//@key` line and what our front end does with it
//   The comment-suffix form is normative, not a vendor dialect: XTypes
//   1.3 Table 24 says an annotation may be applied "alternately and
//   equivalently … by suffixing it with an annotation type name using
//   slash-slash-at". Measured on the tree this file was added to, the
//   form passes `parse` and `lower` without a single diagnostic and
//   `color` comes out with `facets.is_key() == false` — a silent drop
//   at the place where instance keying is decided. The front-end work
//   to read the form is under way.
//
//   The file stays as the suite wrote it either way. A `@key` written
//   in here would make our client agree with a source it does not
//   actually read, and an IDL that differs from the suite's no longer
//   measures the suite.
//
// What checks it
//   `tools/omg-security-main/src/shape_type.rs::tests` — that the
//   generated type announces a `TypeObject`, that its two byte orders
//   are two encodings, and that `HAS_KEY` and `key_hash()` are one
//   decision with `color` behind it.

const long COLOR_MAX_LENGTH=64;

struct ShapeType {
    string<COLOR_MAX_LENGTH> color; //@key
    long x;
    long y;
    long shapesize;
};
